1.What is Pandas?
Pandas is an open-source Python library used for data manipulation and analysis. It provides high-level data structures like DataFrame and Series for handling structured data efficiently.

2.series v/s Datafame :
DataFrame = collection of Series sharing same index
Feature	Series	DataFrame
Dimension	1D	2D
Structure	Single column	Multiple columns
Example	list	table

3. What is an Index in Pandas?
 Index = labels for rows
•	Can be numeric or custom 
•	Immutable (mostly) 
•	Enables fast lookup

4.  What is the difference between loc and iloc?
🔹 loc - 
•	Label-based 
•	Uses column/row names 
🔹 iloc -
•	Position-based 
•	Uses integer index
Example:
df.loc[0, 'name']   # label
df.iloc[0, 1]       # position

5. What is the difference between copy() and view() 

copy () - Creates a completely new object with its own memory.
Changes in copied data → ❌ do NOT affect original
Ex:
df = pd.DataFrame({'A': [1, 2, 3]})
df_copy = df.copy()

View () - Returns a reference to the original data (shares memory).
Changes in view → ✅ affect original DataFrame
Example:
df_view = df['A']   # often behaves like a view
df_view[0] = 100
print(df)


6. What is SettingWithCopyWarning?
Happens when: You modify a slice of DataFrame incorrectly
Ex: df[df['age'] > 25]['salary'] = 5000  ❌
Crct : df.loc[df['age'] > 25, 'salary'] = 5000 ✅

7. What is groupby()?
Used for aggregation  - Steps:
1.	Split 
2.	Apply 
3.	Combine
Ex: df.groupby('dept')['salary'].mean()

8. Difference between apply(), map(), applymap()?

map () : Works only on Series (single column)
Use case: Element-wise transformation
df['salary'] = df['salary'].map(lambda x: x * 2)
✔ Applies function to each value
✔ Can also use dictionary mapping

apply (): Works on Series AND DataFrame
Case 1: On Series
df['salary'].apply(lambda x: x * 2)

Case 2: On DataFrame
Operates on rows or columns
df.apply(lambda row: row['A'] + row['B'], axis=1)

applymap() : Works on: Entire DataFrame
Use case: Element-wise operation on all cells
ex: df.applymap(lambda x: x * 2)



Function	Applies on	Use
map()	Series	element-wise
apply()	Series/DataFrame	row/column
applymap()	DataFrame	element-wise

9. What is merge() vs join() vs concat()
🔹 merge()
•	SQL-like join 
•	Based on keys 
🔹 join () : DataFrame.join() joins two DataFrames based on their index (row labels) by default
•	Index-based merge 
•	Steps :  Join is done on index  , Matching indices → values combined , Non-matching → NaN
🔹 concat()
•	Stack/append data

10. How does Pandas handle missing data?
    isnull() / notnull()  ,  fillna()  ,  dropna()

11. What is vectorization in Pandas?
Operations applied on entire column at once. Faster than loops
Ex: df['salary'] * 2   # vectorized

12. What is the difference between pivot () & pivot_table()?
pivot() → no aggregation 
pivot_table() → supports aggregation

13. What is MultiIndex?
Hierarchical indexing
Used for:
•	Complex data representation

14. Why is Pandas faster than Python loops?
Because:
•	Built on NumPy (C optimized) 
•	Vectorized operations 
•	Efficient memory usage

15. How do you handle large datasets in Pandas?

Use chunking (pd.read_csv(..., chunksize=...)) 
Select only required columns (usecols) 
Optimize dtypes (category, int32 instead of int64) 
Use vectorized operations 
Avoid apply() on large data 
Consider switching to Dask or PySpark for scalability

16. How do you optimize memory usage in Pandas?
Convert object → category 
Downcast numeric types 
Drop unnecessary columns 
Use astype() wisely : general-purpose type conversion method
df['col'] = df['col'].astype('category')

17. Why is apply() slow? What is the alternative?
apply() uses Python-level loops → slow 
Alternatives: 
•	Vectorized operations 
•	NumPy functions 
•	Built-in Pandas methods
df['A'] + df['B']   # faster than apply

18. How do you handle duplicate data?
•	Identify:  : df.duplicated()
•	Remove:  df.drop_duplicates()
•	Keep specific:  df.drop_duplicates(subset=['col'], keep='last')

19. How do you perform window functions in Pandas?
Equivalent of SQL window functions
df['rank'] = df['salary'].rank()
df['running_total'] = df['salary'].cumsum()
Group-based: df.groupby('dept')['salary'].rank()
20. How do you handle skewed data in Pandas?
Use sampling 
Normalize/log transform 
Group aggregation carefully 
Consider distributed systems if skew is large

21. How do you deal with missing data in production pipelines?
•  Identify (isnull) 
•  Decide strategy: 
•	Drop 
•	Fill (mean/median/mode) 
•	Forward fill / backward fill 
•  Validate after transformation

22. How do you merge very large DataFrames efficiently?
•	Ensure join keys are indexed 
•	Reduce columns before merge 
•	Use appropriate join type 
•	Sort data before merge (sometimes helps) 

23. What is vectorization and why is it important?
Applying operations to entire columns at once 
Uses optimized C backend (NumPy) 
Much faster than loops

24. How do you debug performance issues in Pandas?
•	Use .info() and .memory_usage() 
•	Check for: 
o	Unnecessary loops 
o	Large object columns 
o	Inefficient joins 
•	Profile code 

25. When should you NOT use Pandas?
Answer:
•	Very large data (GB–TB scale) 
•	Distributed processing needed 
•	Real-time streaming 
 Use:
•	PySpark 
•	Dask 

26. What is the difference between shallow copy and deep copy?
Shallow → reference 
Deep → independent copy

27. You have a 10GB CSV file. What will you do?
•	Don’t load fully ❌ 
•	Use chunking
for chunk in pd.read_csv('file.csv', chunksize=100000):
    process(chunk)

28. How do you handle schema inconsistencies across multiple CSV files?
Normalize column names (str.lower(), rename) 
Align schema using reindex() or column selection 
Add missing columns with default values 
Enforce schema using dtype=
29. How do you ensure data quality in Pandas pipelines?
Null checks (isnull()) 
Range validation 
Duplicate checks 
Schema validation 
Assertions/logging

30. How do you handle JSON or nested data in Pandas?

•	Use json_normalize() 
import pandas as pd
df = pd.json_normalize(data)
•	Flatten nested structures for analysis

31. How do you handle timezone-aware data?
•	Convert using: 
df['time'] = pd.to_datetime(df['time']).dt.tz_localize('UTC')
•	Use tz_convert() for transformations 

32. What is explode()
Used to transform list-like column into multiple rows
df.explode('col')
Important for semi-structured data

33. How do you handle incremental data processing?

Filter new records using timestamp or ID 
Append to existing dataset 
Avoid full reload


34. How do you optimize join performance?

Set index before join : df.set_index('id')
Reduce dataset size before join
Use appropriate join type
Avoid unnecessary columns

35. What is categorical dtype and when to use it?

Used for low-cardinality columns (like country, gender) 
Saves memory 
Improves performance
Categories → ['NY', 'LA'] 
Data stored as → [0, 1, 0, 1, 0]

36. What is the difference between stack() and unstack()?

Answer: Used in reshaping data
•	stack() → columns → rows 
•	unstack() → rows → columns 

37. How do you monitor memory usage?
df.memory_usage(deep=True)
df.info()

38.What happens internally when you perform a merge in Pandas?

✅ Answer (what interviewer expects)

👉 Pandas uses:

Hash join (most common)
Sometimes sort-merge join (if data is sorted)

👉 Steps:

Build hash table on join key
Match keys from other DataFrame
Combine rows

👉 Important:

Duplicate keys → row multiplication (many-to-many)

